WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.162
Jump, jump, jump. Three blocks
to make the cat jump three times.

2
00:00:08.382 --> 00:00:12.701
Now say it with a repeat block.
Repeat 3 times, jump. Two blocks.

3
00:00:13.201 --> 00:00:18.309
An algorithm is the plan. It is
the steps, written for a person.

4
00:00:18.529 --> 00:00:23.080
Move right. Jump. Say hello.
That is an algorithm, in words.

5
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:28.083
A program is the same plan, built out
of blocks the computer can follow.

6
00:00:28.303 --> 00:00:31.740
The move right block, the jump
block, the say hello block.

7
00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:36.604
Now a computer can run it. The
program says how to run the algorithm.

8
00:00:37.104 --> 00:00:41.051
Write the algorithm first, in
words. Then build it in blocks.

9
00:00:41.271 --> 00:00:45.730
Take each step of the algorithm
and place its block, in order.

10
00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:50.083
Jump, jump, move left. Two jump
blocks, then the move left block.

11
00:00:50.303 --> 00:00:54.389
Then press Run. The cat does
the blocks one at a time.

12
00:00:54.609 --> 00:00:58.928
Watching it run is how you test
it. Did the cat do exactly that?

13
00:00:59.428 --> 00:01:03.283
Here is a new kind of block.
This one is the repeat block.

14
00:01:03.503 --> 00:01:07.032
The repeat block repeats the
block that comes after it.

15
00:01:07.252 --> 00:01:12.639
Repeat 3 times, jump. The cat
jumps three times, from two blocks.

16
00:01:12.859 --> 00:01:16.435
The repeat block always goes
before the block it repeats.

17
00:01:16.655 --> 00:01:21.578
Put it after the jump, and nothing is
repeated. It needs a block after it.

18
00:01:21.798 --> 00:01:26.953
Some apps have a repeat that holds several
blocks. This one repeats just the next one.

19
00:01:27.453 --> 00:01:31.864
Jump, jump, jump and repeat 3 times,
jump do exactly the same thing.

20
00:01:32.084 --> 00:01:37.007
The output is what a program makes
happen. Three jumps, both ways.

21
00:01:37.227 --> 00:01:42.057
The second program is shorter, and
shorter is easier to read and fix.

22
00:01:42.277 --> 00:01:46.178
Careful, though. Repeat 2 times,
move right is only two moves.

23
00:01:46.398 --> 00:01:50.810
Move right, move right, move right
is three. Count what the cat does.

24
00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:54.839
Build ten blocks, run it once
at the end, and it goes wrong.

25
00:01:55.059 --> 00:01:59.517
The bug could be in any of the
ten. That is ten places to look.

26
00:01:59.737 --> 00:02:04.474
Now build two blocks, run it, check.
Build two more, and run it again.

27
00:02:04.694 --> 00:02:08.084
If it goes wrong now, the bug
can only be in the last two.

28
00:02:08.304 --> 00:02:13.227
Programmers test regularly, all the way
through. The bug is in the newest bit.

29
00:02:13.727 --> 00:02:18.371
These blocks work like the ones in
ScratchJr, a real app on a tablet.

30
00:02:18.591 --> 00:02:23.049
You choose a character, you choose a
background, then you drag blocks together.

31
00:02:23.269 --> 00:02:28.842
Your repeat 3 times is a loop block
with a 3 in it. The green flag is Go.

32
00:02:29.342 --> 00:02:32.732
A program tells the computer
how to run an algorithm.

33
00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:36.528
The repeat block goes
before the block it repeats.

34
00:02:36.748 --> 00:02:40.742
Same output, fewer blocks. And
test as you go, every time.
